## Strengths

S1. **First time-space tradeoffs for KDE data structures.** To the best of the authors' knowledge, this is the first work providing a smooth tradeoff between query time and space for Gaussian KDE data structures. This is a significant structural contribution, as prior work only gave fixed operating points. The parameterization via δ ≥ 0 yielding query time 1/µ^{ξ(δ)} and space 1/µ^{1+δ} provides a unified framework that subsumes and extends prior constructions.

S2. **Significant improvement in query time exponent.** The query time of 1/µ^{0.051} (albeit at polynomial space) represents a substantial improvement over the previous best of 1/µ^{0.173} from Charikar et al. (2020). Even in the linear space regime (δ=0), the query time exponent of 0.1865 improves over the data-independent bound of 0.25 from the same prior work.

S3. **Elegant use of asymmetric LSH.** The key insight—that the symmetric LSH framework of Charikar et al. (2020) can be strictly improved by deploying the asymmetric LSH of Andoni et al. (2017) because the bottleneck scale for query time differs from the bottleneck scale for space—is clean and well-motivated. This observation connects two important strands of the ANN literature in a natural way.

S4. **Clear identification of a fundamental barrier.** The paper honestly discusses the barrier at ≈ 1/µ^{0.09} (even with unlimited space, setting ρ_q=0) and ≈ 1/µ^{0.05} (with optimized ρ_q), posing an explicit open problem about whether constant query time KDE in polynomial space is achievable. This is a valuable conceptual contribution.
